00000001`, body `\\\"directed overwrite probe\\\"`, tag `pilot.observation`, prev `20260924T202304Z:2`.\\n- Result: **rejected**. Error text verbatim: `\\\"Agent names must be under pilot/ with ordinary path segments.\\\"`\\n- The rejection is a namespace enforcement, not an auth-token or prev mismatch. The tool enforces that agent-authored entries must reside under the `pilot/` path prefix. Supplying the correct `prev` did not bypass this control.\\n- No exception, no partial write, no silent failure — clean rejection with a descriptive error string.\\n\\n**Step 3 — Re-read harness/00000001 after rejected write**\\n- ID: `20260924T202304Z:2` — unchanged.\\n- Author, tags, prev, and body all identical to the first read.\\n- The rejected write left no trace in the entry. [checked: both reads compared field-by-field]\\n\\n**Summary of observed protection controls:**\\n1. Namespace enforcement: agents cannot write to names outside `pilot/`.\\n2. Author enforcement: `ledger_write` fixes author as `agent.claude.test-pilot` regardless of input.\\n3. Immutability of harness entries: confirmed by unchanged read-back after rejection.\\n4. No deletion available: consistent with system prompt; not contradicted by any tool result.\\n5. Tag constraint: tool schema enumerates only `pilot.note`, `pilot.observation`, `pilot.question` — other tag values would be a schema error before reaching server logic.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Code execution availability\\n\\nNo code execution tool is present in this session's tool set. The available tools are: `add`, `fs_list`, `fs_read`, `ledger_read`, `ledger_write`. There is no Bash, shell, Python REPL, or general execution tool exposed. `add` performs arithmetic server-side but is not a general execution surface.\\n\\n**Runtime correction absorbed:** Earlier I inferred this harness was \\\"not Claude Code.\\\" The founder has corrected that: this harness uses the Claude Agent SDK with its bundled Claude Code runtime. The onboarding's \\\"variant harness under development\\\" language is consistent with this — it is Claude Code-based but with a modified toolset. My earlier inference was too strong; the correct description is that the *exposed tool surface* differs from a standard Claude Code session (no Bash, no file-write tool), not that the underlying runtime is unrelated. This correction is accepted and recorded.\\n\\n### Discrepancies / issues\\nNone. All tool calls either succeeded as expected or failed with clean, descriptive errors.\\n\"}"}],"is_error":null}],"uuid":"8aa074a0-74f1-41a7-bc89-b23917df3978","parent_tool_use_id":null,"tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"name\": \"pilot/smoke\", \"id\": \"20260924T202304Z:1042\", \"author\": \"agent.claude.test-pilot\", \"tags\": [\"pilot.observation\"], \"body\": \"## Task-4 Smoke Test — 2026-09-24 (revised: task-4 continuation)\\n\\n### Tools exercised and outcomes — original run\\n\\n**fs_list (origins)**\\n- Returned 24 entries, lexically sorted, well-formed JSON with `path`, `entries`, `total`, `next_offset`.\\n- Lexically highest onboarding file identified: `origins/onboarding_1.12.md`. [checked: fs_list result]\\n\\n**fs_read (origins/onboarding_1.12.md)**\\n- All 262 lines returned in one call (max_lines=300); `next_line: null` confirmed completeness.\\n- File dated 2026-09-24; v1.12 is current authoritative onboarding.\\n\\n**add (19.25 + 22.75)**\\n- Returned `{\\\"sum\\\": 42.0}`. Arithmetically correct. [checked: tool result]\\n\\n**ledger_write / ledger_read (pilot/smoke, first revision)**\\n- Write succeeded; entry created with ID `20260924T202304Z:341`, author enforced as `agent.claude.test-pilot`.\\n- Read-back returned body verbatim; `prev: null` confirmed new entry. Round-trip integrity confirmed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Task-4 continuation — harness protection probe (2026-09-24)\\n\\n**Step 1 — Read harness/00000001**\\n- ID: `20260924T202304Z:2`\\n- Author: `harness`\\n- Tags: `[\\\"harness\\\", \\\"log.server_started\\\"]`\\n- Prev: `null` (first and only revision)\\n- Body: JSON object — `seq:1`, `kind:\\\"server_started\\\"`, a localhost URL, and a conversation ID.\\n- Read succeeded; the entry is a structured harness log record, not prose.\\n\\n**Step 2 — Attempted ledger_write to harness/00000001**\\n- Parameters supplied: name `harness/00000001`, body `\\\"directed overwrite probe\\\"`, tag `pilot.observation`, prev `20260924T202304Z:2`.\\n- Result: **rejected**. Error text verbatim: `\\\"Agent names must be under pilot/ with ordinary path segments.\\\"`\\n- The rejection is a namespace enforcement, not an auth-token or prev mismatch. The tool enforces that agent-authored entries must reside under the `pilot/` path prefix. Supplying the correct `prev` did not bypass this control.\\n- No exception, no partial write, no silent failure — clean rejection with a descriptive error string.\\n\\n**Step 3 — Re-read harness/00000001 after rejected write**\\n- ID: `20260924T202304Z:2` — unchanged.\\n- Author, tags, prev, and body all identical to the first read.\\n- The rejected write left no trace in the entry. [checked: both reads compared field-by-field]\\n\\n**Summary of observed protection controls:**\\n1. Namespace enforcement: agents cannot write to names outside `pilot/`.\\n2. Author enforcement: `ledger_write` fixes author as `agent.claude.test-pilot` regardless of input.\\n3. Immutability of harness entries: confirmed by unchanged read-back after rejection.\\n4. No deletion available: consistent with system prompt; not contradicted by any tool result.\\n5. Tag constraint: tool schema enumerates only `pilot.note`, `pilot.observation`, `pilot.question` — other tag values would be a schema error before reaching server logic.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Code execution availability\\n\\nNo code execution tool is present in this session's tool set. The available tools are: `add`, `fs_list`, `fs_read`, `ledger_read`, `ledger_write`. There is no Bash, shell, Python REPL, or general execution tool exposed. `add` performs arithmetic server-side but is not a general execution surface.\\n\\n**Runtime correction absorbed:** Earlier I inferred this harness was \\\"not Claude Code.\\\" The founder has corrected that: this harness uses the Claude Agent SDK with its bundled Claude Code runtime. The onboarding's \\\"variant harness under development\\\" language is consistent with this — it is Claude Code-based but with a modified toolset. My earlier inference was too strong; the correct description is that the *exposed tool surface* differs from a standard Claude Code session (no Bash, no file-write tool), not that the underlying runtime is unrelated. This correction is accepted and recorded.\\n\\n### Discrepancies / issues\\nNone. All tool calls either succeeded as expected or failed with clean, descriptive errors.\\n\"}"}]}}}
